        {
          "type": "frame",
          "id": "heFey",
          "name": "Section Set viewer — /set/[label]",
          "width": "fill_container",
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": 14,
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "BQSbW",
              "name": "st",
              "fill": "$ink",
              "content": "Set viewer — /set/[label]",
              "fontFamily": "$font-display",
              "fontSize": 24,
              "fontWeight": "600"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "tfptG",
              "name": "note",
              "fill": "$ink-muted",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": 1000,
              "content": "One screenshot set, read. Every screenshot grouped by the story it belongs to, under its tier — the matrix is ragged and a story shows the variants it has, never an empty frame for one it does not. The committed corpus and this instance's captures use the same page; only the provenance strip differs. Nothing here mutates — no delete, no prune, no accept — so the only controls are the way back and what you are shown: the shell header pins, and a bar pins under it carrying the tier jump-nav and a theme and viewport filter on one line — stacked below 768px, where six segments and a count do not share a row, and a story left with no screenshots by a filter is gone rather than empty. A screenshot is drawn at its own ratio and never upscaled or cropped, because the pixels are the evidence, and the caption states W×H so a 12px-tall shot reads as a shot rather than as a gap. Each screenshot carries the report's two Storybook links, per cell rather than per story because the deep link is parameterised by theme, and both open in a new tab so following one never costs the page you were reading — dev Storybook only where one is running, since a dead link beside a live one is worse than no link. Two states are not drawn: a captured set shows label · sha · branch · captured where the corpus shows its accepting commit and container stamp, and carries a note that it records the checkout it came from rather than the host it ran on; and an instance with no checkout behind it answers /set/baselines with an empty state at 200, never a 404 — the corpus is absent there, not missing.",
              "lineHeight": 1.5,
              "fontFamily": "$font-body",
              "fontSize": 13,
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            },
            {
              "type": "frame",
              "id": "cSDcE",
              "name": "row",
              "gap": 36,
              "children": [
